WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.103
Here's homework problem one.

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:08.681
A box, five hundred millimeters wide and three hundred tall.

00:00:08.681 --> 00:00:15.002
The top and bottom skins and the left web are ten millimeters thick.

00:00:15.002 --> 00:00:17.140
The right web is eight.

00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:25.632
Each corner has an angle, a little L shaped stiffener.

00:00:25.632 --> 00:00:29.157
Sixty by fifty by ten on the left.

00:00:29.157 --> 00:00:32.993
Fifty by forty by eight on the right.

00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:41.533
The problem asks us to replace all this with four corner booms, so let's add up what goes into each boom.

00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:42.547
First the angles.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.302
An angle's area is the two leg lengths, minus the overlap, times the thickness.

00:00:49.302 --> 00:00:55.880
Sixty plus fifty minus ten is one hundred, times ten: one thousand square millimeters on the left.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:05.720
Fifty plus forty minus eight is eighty two, times eight: six hundred fifty six on the right.

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:07.441
Next the skins.

00:01:07.441 --> 00:01:12.377
Top and bottom skins have the same stress at both ends, so the ratio is one.

00:01:12.377 --> 00:01:17.507
Each gives ten times five hundred over two: twenty five hundred to each corner.

00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:19.248
Last, the webs.

00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:24.314
They cross the neutral axis, so the ratio is minus one.

00:01:24.314 --> 00:01:30.392
The left web gives ten times three hundred over six: five hundred.

00:01:30.392 --> 00:01:36.747
The right web gives eight times three hundred over six: four hundred.

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:48.593
Adding it all up: each left boom is four thousand square millimeters, and each right boom is three thousand five hundred fifty six.

00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:57.098
Number the booms: one at the top left, then clockwise: two, three, four.

00:01:57.098 --> 00:02:02.087
Every boom is one hundred fifty millimeters above or below the middle.

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:08.033
The box is symmetric top to bottom, so I x y is zero.

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:23.702
I x x is each boom area times one fifty squared, added up: two times four thousand plus three thousand five hundred fifty six, times one fifty squared.

00:02:23.702 --> 00:02:27.334
That's three hundred forty million.

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:29.558
We want the shear centre.

00:02:29.558 --> 00:02:34.647
So put a vertical load S y at an unknown distance xi from the left web.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.323
Cut the top wall.

00:02:36.323 --> 00:02:39.983
Then go around in order: one, two, three, four.

00:02:39.983 --> 00:02:43.720
Wall one two starts at zero, because of the cut.

00:02:44.034 --> 00:02:54.910
Crossing boom two: minus S y over I x x, times three five five six, times one fifty.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:03:06.434
That's minus one point five six nine times ten to the minus three, S y, in the right web.

00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:16.167
Boom three is one fifty below the middle, so it cancels that exactly: the bottom wall is zero.

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:29.574
Boom four is also below the middle, and bigger: the left web gets plus one point seven six five times ten to the minus three, S y.

00:03:31.999 --> 00:03:34.774
Now close the cut with a constant q s zero.

00:03:34.774 --> 00:03:38.840
Because the load is at the shear centre, the box doesn't twist.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:44.519
With one material, that means the integral of q d s over t, all the way around, is zero.

00:03:44.866 --> 00:03:48.415
d s over t is each wall's length over its thickness.

00:03:48.415 --> 00:03:50.736
Top: five hundred over ten, fifty.

00:03:50.736 --> 00:03:54.626
Right: three hundred over eight, thirty seven and a half.

00:03:55.532 --> 00:03:57.250
Bottom: fifty again.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:00.428
Left: three hundred over ten, thirty.

00:04:00.428 --> 00:04:04.292
In total, one hundred sixty seven and a half.

00:04:04.632 --> 00:04:08.411
Only the two webs have basic flow.

00:04:08.411 --> 00:04:27.192
So: minus one point five six nine times thirty seven and a half, plus one point seven six five times thirty, plus one sixty seven and a half times q s zero, equals zero.

00:04:27.532 --> 00:04:33.707
The first two terms add up to minus five point eight nine times ten to the minus three.

00:04:33.707 --> 00:04:38.604
So q s zero is three point five two times ten to the minus five, S y.

00:04:38.604 --> 00:04:41.372
A small correction, all the way around.

00:04:41.732 --> 00:04:45.252
Add it to every wall to get the final shear flows.

00:04:47.632 --> 00:04:49.833
Both webs now carry shear flow upward.

00:04:49.833 --> 00:04:53.712
Multiply by their height, three hundred millimeters, to get forces.

00:04:54.032 --> 00:05:03.357
The right web carries zero point four six S y, and the left web zero point five four S y.

00:05:03.357 --> 00:05:05.663
Together, exactly S y.

00:05:05.663 --> 00:05:08.072
Good, that's our check.

00:05:08.999 --> 00:05:11.439
Now take moments about the left web.

00:05:11.439 --> 00:05:16.927
The right web's force, zero point four six S y, is five hundred millimeters away.

00:05:16.927 --> 00:05:18.959
That's two hundred thirty S y.

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:28.219
The small flows in the top and bottom skins point opposite ways, so they make a small couple, the other way: about five point three S y.

00:05:28.499 --> 00:05:37.024
So S y times xi equals two thirty point zero four, minus five point two seven.

00:05:37.024 --> 00:05:44.892
xi is two hundred twenty four point eight millimeters from the left web.

00:05:44.892 --> 00:05:46.859
That's the answer.

00:05:47.665 --> 00:05:55.505
It sits left of the middle, because the left booms are heavier and carry more of the load.

00:05:55.965 --> 00:05:56.827
Your turn.

00:05:56.827 --> 00:06:03.805
Pause here and do checkpoint four point one: the same steps for the practice box.
